#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186192391 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186192686 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "a9d0e7a8-77d1-58d7-b7f7-a820942ece69" :knowledge-point/title "Log-Likelihood Functions for Normal Random Variables"}] :question/id #uuid "19e7744e-9d87-5ad1-9648-029e7dc8e565" :question/math-academy-id "e-10113" :question/problem "Suppose $X_1,X_2,\\ldots,X_{8}$ is an I.I.D random sample with $X_i \\sim N(\\theta, 1^2)$, where $\\theta$ is an unknown mean. For a particular sample $x_1, x_2, \\ldots, x_{8}$, you're given that\n$\\sum_{i=1}^{8} x_i = 11.8, \\qquad \\sum_{i=1}^{8} x_i^2 = 25.2$.\nFind the log-likelihood function of the sample.\n\n*Hint: The probability density function for a normal random variable with mean $\\theta$ and standard deviation $1$ is given by*\n$f(x) = \\dfrac{1}{\\sqrt{2\\pi}} e^{-(x-\\theta)^2/2}, \\qquad x \\in (-\\infty,\\infty)$." :question/worked-solution "Recall that if $X_1, X_2, \\ldots, X_{n}$ are independent and identically distributed continuous random variables with probability density function $f(x; \\theta)$, where $\\theta$ is an unknown parameter, then the log-likelihood function of a random sample $x_1, x_2, \\ldots, x_n$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nl(θ) &= \\ln L(θ) \\\\\n&= \\ln (∏_(i = 1)^(n)f(x_{i} \\\\\n&= θ)) \\\\\n&= ∑_(i = 1)^(n)\\ln (f(x_{i} \\\\\n&= θ))\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIn our case, we are given a sample of $n=8$ independent normal random variables with unknown mean $\\theta$ and variance\n\n$$\n\\sigma^2 = 1\n$$\n\nfor which the probability density function is\n\n$$\nf(x, \\theta) = \\dfrac{1}{\\sqrt{2\\pi}} e^{-(x-\\theta)^2/2}, \\qquad x \\in (-\\infty,\\infty)\n$$\n\nFirst, let's write down the log-density function:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\ln f(x, θ) &= \\ln (\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2π}}e^{- (x - θ)^{2}/2}) \\\\\n&= \\ln (\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2π}}) + \\ln (e^{- (x - θ)^{2}/2}) \\\\\n&=-\\frac{1}{2}\\ln (2π) - ((x - θ)^{2})/(2)\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we can calculate the log-likelihood function as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nl(θ) &= ∑_(i = 1)^(n)(-\\frac{1}{2}\\ln (2π) - ((x_{i} - θ)^{2})/(2)) \\\\\n&=-\\frac{n}{2}\\ln (2π) - ∑_(i = 1)^(n)((x_{i} - θ)^{2})/(2) \\\\\n&=-\\frac{n}{2}\\ln (2π) - \\frac{1}{2}(∑_(i = 1)^(n)x_{i}^{2} - 2θ∑_(i = 1)^(n)x_{i} + nθ^{2})\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe are told that\n\n$$\n\\displaystyle\\sum_{i=1}^{8} x_i = 11.8\n$$\n\n$$\n\\displaystyle\\sum_{i=1}^{8} x_i^2 = 25.2\n$$\n\nand $n=8$. Therefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nl(θ) &= -\\frac{8}{2}\\ln (2π) - \\frac{1}{2}(25.2 - 2θ \\cdot 11.8 + 8θ^{2}) \\\\\n&=-4\\ln (2π) - 12.6 + 11.8θ - 4θ^{2}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186192522 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186192798 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "7981ed99-ef47-5b86-9f92-bce4d45d9767" :knowledge-point/title "Log-Likelihood Functions for Exponential Random Variables"}] :question/id #uuid "f2a062b5-e42e-5c05-9bc1-cbbe7a10baac" :question/math-academy-id "e-11122" :question/problem "Suppose $X_1, X_2, X_3, X_4, X_5$ is an I.I.D random sample with $X_i \\sim \\text{Exp}(\\theta)$ and unknown rate parameter $\\theta$. Find the log-likelihood function of the sample $2, \\, 4, \\, 1, \\, 3, \\, 7$." :question/worked-solution "Recall that if $X_1, X_2, \\ldots, X_{n}$ are independent and identically distributed continuous random variables with probability density function $f(x; \\theta)$, where $\\theta$ is an unknown parameter, then the log-likelihood function of a random sample $x_1, x_2, \\ldots, x_n$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nl(θ) &= \\ln L(θ) \\\\\n&= \\ln (∏_(i = 1)^(n)f(x_{i} \\\\\n&= θ)) \\\\\n&= ∑_(i = 1)^(n)\\ln (f(x_{i} \\\\\n&= θ))\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIn our case, we are given a sample of $n = 5$ independent exponential random variables with unknown parameter $\\theta$, for which the probability density function is\n\n$$\nf(x, \\theta) = \\theta e^{-\\theta x}, \\qquad x \\geq 0\n$$\n\nFirst, let's write down the log-density function:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\ln f(x, θ) &= \\ln (θe^{- θx}) \\\\\n&= \\ln θ + \\ln (e^{- θx}) \\\\\n&= \\ln θ - θx\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we can calculate the log-likelihood function as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nl(θ) &= ∑_(i = 1)^(n)(\\ln θ - θx_{i}) \\\\\n&= ∑_(i = 1)^(n)\\ln θ - θ∑_(i = 1)^(n)x_{i} \\\\\n&= n\\ln θ - θ∑_(i = 1)^(n)x_{i}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFor our sample, we have that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n∑_(i = 1)^(n)x_{i} &= 2 + 4 + 1 + 3 + 7 = 17\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nand $n = 5$. Therefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nl(θ) &= 5\\ln θ - θ \\cdot 17 \\\\\n&= 5\\ln θ - 17θ\n\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186192524 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186192800 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "cce53189-83e8-5ab9-9abc-cb159f761f55" :knowledge-point/title "Log-Likelihood Functions for Continuous Random Variables"}] :question/id #uuid "ac9e9734-fb34-59e4-bbe2-a2e55196f0c1" :question/math-academy-id "e-11124" :question/problem "Let $X_1, X_2, X_3,X_4$ be I.I.D. random variables with probability density function\n$f(x) = \\theta x^{-\\theta-1}, \\qquad x \\geq 1$,\nwhere $\\theta$ is an unknown parameter. Find the log-likelihood function of the sample $1.3, \\, 2.4, \\, 1.0,\\, 1.7$." :question/worked-solution "Recall that if $X_1, X_2, \\ldots, X_{n}$ are independent and identically distributed continuous random variables with probability density function $f(x; \\theta)$, where $\\theta$ is an unknown parameter, then the log-likelihood function of a random sample $x_1, x_2, \\ldots, x_n$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nl(θ) &= \\ln L(θ) \\\\\n&= \\ln (∏_(i = 1)^(n)f(x_{i} \\\\\n&= θ)) \\\\\n&= ∑_(i = 1)^(n)\\ln (f(x_{i} \\\\\n&= θ))\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIn our case, we are given a sample of $n=4$ independent random variables with unknown parameter $\\theta$, for which the probability density function is\n\n$$\nf(x) = \\theta x^{-\\theta-1}, \\qquad x \\geq 1\n$$\n\nFirst, let's write down the log-density function:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\ln f(x \\\\\nθ) &= \\ln (θx^{- θ - 1}) \\\\\n&= \\ln θ + \\ln (x^{- (θ + 1)}) \\\\\n&= \\ln θ - (θ + 1)\\ln x\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we can calculate the log-likelihood function as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nl(θ) &= ∑_(i = 1)^(n)(\\ln θ - (θ + 1)\\ln x_{i}) \\\\\n&= ∑_(i = 1)^(n)\\ln θ - ∑_(i = 1)^(n)(θ + 1)\\ln x_{i} \\\\\n&= \\ln θ∑_(i = 1)^(n)1 - (θ + 1)∑_(i = 1)^(n)\\ln x_{i} \\\\\n&= n\\ln θ - (θ + 1)∑_(i = 1)^(n)\\ln x_{i}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFor our sample, we have that $n=4$, and\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n∑_(i = 1)^(n)\\ln x_{i} &= \\ln 1.3 + \\ln 2.4 + \\ln 1.0 + \\ln 1.7 \\approx 1.67\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\nl(θ) \\mid \\approx 4\\ln θ - (θ + 1) \\cdot 1.67; = 4\\ln θ - 1.67(θ + 1)\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186193199 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186192798 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "7981ed99-ef47-5b86-9f92-bce4d45d9767" :knowledge-point/title "Log-Likelihood Functions for Exponential Random Variables"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "902ee92f-f1e4-5b40-ad45-fc0f0984c13a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "28.4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186191081} {:answer/id #uuid "11a2f3bd-741f-5731-abd0-7524e672a87c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-22.2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186191082} {:answer/id #uuid "2c428dd7-aca3-59aa-800a-c4a8f9e04ebe" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "26.2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186191083} {:answer/id #uuid "49f15e7f-8246-5744-b09b-1ffcb2a7c235" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-20.4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186191084} {:answer/id #uuid "71b39c2e-386a-5eee-9485-d538eff24c0f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-28.4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186191085}] :answer-field/id #uuid "085d6d7c-e590-5e18-8b19-c31691226d38" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186191080}] :question/id #uuid "2d68b12a-1734-50e8-9980-7be41ad6608b" :question/math-academy-id "q-159834" :question/problem "Suppose $X_{1}, X_{2}, …, X_{8}$ is an I.I.D random sample with $X_{i}∼Exp(θ)$ and unknown rate parameter $θ$. For a particular sample $x_{1}, x_{2}, …, x_{8}$, you're given that\n$∑_(i = 1)^(8)x_{i} = 28.4$.\nGiven that the log-likelihood function of the sample is\n$l(θ) = a\\ln θ + bθ$,\nwhat is the value of $a + b$?\n\n*Hint: The probability density function for an exponential random variable is given by*\n$f(x) = θe^{- θx}, x \\ge 0$.\n\n> A scientific calculator is required to answer this question." :question/requires-calculator true}]}
